[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Testing, Mocking, and Dependency Injection in Object-Oriented Programming"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Writing robust, maintainable, and scalable software in Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) requires a strong emphasis on testability. Modern software architecture relies heavily on Dependency Injection (DI) and Mocking to isolate components and ensure unit tests run reliably, quickly, and independently of external systems like databases or network APIs."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Dependency Injection (DI)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Dependency Injection is a design pattern used to achieve Inversion of Control (IoC) between classes and their dependencies. Instead of a class creating its own dependencies internally using the 'new' keyword, those dependencies are passed (injected) into the class from the outside, typically via the constructor."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// Without Dependency Injection (Tight Coupling)
public class OrderProcessor {
    private SqlDatabase database;

    public OrderProcessor() {
        this.database = new SqlDatabase(); // Hardcoded dependency
    }

    public void process(Order order) {
        database.save(order);
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, 'OrderProcessor' is tightly coupled to 'SqlDatabase'. Testing 'OrderProcessor' would require a live SQL database, making tests slow and brittle. We can fix this by introducing an interface and applying Dependency Injection."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"// With Dependency Injection (Loose Coupling)
public interface Database {
    void save(Order order);
}

public class OrderProcessor {
    private final Database database;

    // Dependency is injected through the constructor
    public OrderProcessor(Database database) {
        this.database = database;
    }

    public void process(Order order) {
        database.save(order);
    }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"The Role of Mocking in Unit Testing"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Because 'OrderProcessor' now depends on the 'Database' interface rather than a concrete implementation, we can easily write unit tests. Mocking frameworks allow us to create fake implementations (mocks or stubs) of interfaces during tests, enabling us to verify behavior without hitting real infrastructure."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"// Example Unit Test using a Mock
@Test
public void testOrderProcessing() {
    // 1. Arrange: Create a mock of the Database interface
    Database mockDatabase = Mockito.mock(Database.class);
    OrderProcessor processor = new OrderProcessor(mockDatabase);
    Order order = new Order("123");

    // 2. Act: Call the method under test
    processor.process(order);

    // 3. Assert: Verify that the mock interaction occurred as expected
    Mockito.verify(mockDatabase, Mockito.times(1)).save(order);
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for DI and Mocking in OOP"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Program to interfaces, not implementations: Always inject abstractions (interfaces or abstract classes) rather than concrete classes."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Prefer constructor injection: Constructor injection makes dependencies mandatory and immutable, preventing objects from entering an invalid state."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid the Service Locator pattern: Service Locators hide dependencies and make unit testing harder; stick to explicit dependency injection."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep mocks focused: Only mock types that you own or interfaces at system boundaries to prevent fragile tests."}]
